            <ArticleTitle>An unusual case of stroke.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>229-32</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/NRL.0b013e31825bbf4d</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>New imaging techniques have allowed for the rapid and accurate diagnosis of stroke. In this case, we present a 58-year-old woman with multiple large vessel strokes on magnetic resonance imaging. The initial diagnostic workup centered on a rapidly progressive central nervous system vasculitis. Subsequent workup revealed an unusual infectious etiology--cryptococcal meningitis. Although fungal infections can cause vasculitis, this is the first report of a patient with multiple anterior and posterior circulation strokes secondary to Cryptococcus. The diagnosis in cases presenting with encepalopathy and without fever is often delayed.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
